--- FFmpeg.orig/doc/filters.texi
+++ FFmpeg/doc/filters.texi
@@ -2173,6 +2173,68 @@ agapfill=start=0:max_gap=600
 @end example
 @end itemize
 
+@subsection Commands
+
+This filter supports the following commands:
+@table @option
+@item position
+Report where the output stands, as four numbers with slashes between them:
+the timestamp of the next output sample, the end of the last input frame that
+the filter has output, the end of the last input frame that it has taken, the
+frames that wait on its input included, all three in samples, and the sample
+rate. Each end goes by that frame's own timestamp. Until the filter has output
+an input frame, the second number is the smallest timestamp, so a filter that
+resumes from the report fills the time up to its first frame with silence,
+however short that time is, as it does after @option{start}. The report is
+empty before the first frame. It takes up to 74 bytes, the terminating null
+included, and the command fails when the space for the reply is shorter.
+
+@item pending
+Report how many input frames the filter has not output yet: the frame it
+holds while it writes the silence before it, and the frames that wait behind
+that one. Once a filter behind it has ended its output, for example
+@code{atrim}, no frame can leave, and the filter drops the frame it holds
+and reports none.
+
+@item resume
+Continue the output of another agapfill filter. The argument is what the
+@code{position} command of that filter reported. The filter then ignores
+@option{start}, and its first frame is one more step after the last frame the
+other filter output: a gap between the two is filled, a step at or below
+@option{threshold} passes through, and a longer overlap is joined. When the
+input ends before a frame comes, the end of the last frame the other filter
+took is that step, so the time of the frames it took and did not output
+becomes silence there too.
+A position that no timestamp at the filter's own sample rate reaches stops at
+the largest or the smallest timestamp, as the output position does. The
+command has no effect once the filter has taken a frame.
+@end table
+
+@command{ffmpeg} builds a new filter graph when the parameters of an input
+change mid-stream, for example the channel layout of the audio. Each agapfill
+filter of the new graph then resumes from the position of the filter in its
+place in the old graph. A filter whose predecessor took no frame starts
+afresh, with @option{start}.
+
+Before @command{ffmpeg} frees the old graph, it asks the outputs behind each
+agapfill filter for frames until the filter has output every frame it has, so
+a rebuild while the filter writes silence loses neither the rest of the
+silence nor a sample. It stops asking as soon as one of those outputs gives no
+frame, because a filter behind agapfill waits for another input of the graph,
+for example @code{amix}. The frames the filter still holds are then lost, and
+the filter of the new graph writes their time as silence, so the audio after
+them keeps its own time, and the output reaches their end when no audio
+follows them. Samples that other filters of the old graph hold are lost, as
+they are without this filter. An output that takes no more frames, for example
+after @option{-frames}, discards what it is given, so the frames it gives do
+not keep the asking going. It is still asked, and stops the asking when it
+gives no frame, because a filter on its way may wait as well.
+
+While @command{ffmpeg} asks for the rest of a gap, the other inputs of the
+graph wait. With @option{-shortest}, the audio of an output can then run past
+its video, to about the end of the gap less @option{-shortest_buf_duration}.
+@option{max_gap} bounds that.
+
